# =============================================================================
# Argos DHCPv6 Option Request Option Database (dhcp6_oro.db)
# =============================================================================
# Syntax Format (Pipe-delimited):
# regex_pattern|OS_Name|Score|Device_Type|Vendor
#
# Matching input:
#   The raw ordered DHCPv6 ORO option-code list emitted by argos-sniffer,
#   for example: 23,24,82
#
# Confidence policy:
#   ORO ordering is useful fingerprint context, but common option sets are not
#   unique enough to assert a physical device type or OS by themselves. Broad
#   initial signatures therefore stay below the strong-election threshold and
#   use Device_Type=generic. Exact device/OS signatures can be promoted only
#   after validation against repeatable captures from known clients.
#
# Notes:
#   - Order is intentionally significant; do not sort the observed ORO.
#   - RFC 7844 explicitly notes that ORO contents/order can fingerprint clients.
#   - Modern DHCPv6 clients commonly request SOL_MAX_RT (option 82), so option
#     82 alone must never be treated as an OS fingerprint.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Documented / conservative client-stack context
# =============================================================================
# ISC dhclient documents DNS recursive name servers (23) and domain search (24)
# as its default DHCPv6 request set. Keep this contextual because dhclient is
# configurable and is available on multiple Unix-like platforms.
^23,24$|ISC DHCPv6 Client|60|generic|ISC

# Common modern DNS/search request plus SOL_MAX_RT. This is deliberately broad
# and must not vote in OS/type election.
^23,24,82$|Modern DHCPv6 DNS/Search Client|55|generic|Unknown

# SOL_MAX_RT alone is protocol behavior, not a platform identity. Android's
# DHCPv6 PD client is one known implementation that requests it, but RFC 8415
# also makes SOL_MAX_RT generally relevant to compliant clients.
^82$|DHCPv6 SOL_MAX_RT Client|45|generic|Unknown

# Microsoft documents an Information-request case where USER_CLASS (15) may be
# the only requested option. Keep the signature generic until observed traffic
# proves a stable Windows-only pattern in Argos captures.
^15$|DHCPv6 User-Class Request Client|50|generic|Unknown
